>>> Explain step by step, in about 150 words, why the sky is blue during the day and red at sunset.
The sky appears blue during the day due to a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight contains all colors of the visible spectrum, but shorter wavelengths like blue scatter more easily when hitting gas molecules in the atmosphere than longer wavelengths like red. This scattered blue light reaches our eyes from all directions, making the sky look blue.

At sunset, the sun is lower on the horizon, so sunlight must travel through a much thicker layer of the atmosphere to reach us. During this longer journey, most of the blue light is scattered away completely. The remaining light, dominated by longer wavelengths like red, orange, and yellow, passes through to our eyes. Consequently, the sun and the surrounding sky take on warm, reddish hues. This dramatic shift in color is purely a result of the angle of the sun and the amount of atmospheric scattering occurring.
